</div>
<h2>Controlling</h2>
<p>The basic procedure for writing text to the display is as follows:</p>
<ol>
<li>Set <span class=3D"overline">WR</span>, <span class=3D"overline">CLR</s=
pan> and <span class=3D"overline">CU</span> to 1.</li>
<li>Set CUE to 0.</li>
<li>Set <span class=3D"overline">CE1</span> and <span class=3D"overline">CE=
2</span> to 0 to activate the module (wenn you only use one module, you can=
 also connect these inputs to GND permanently).</li>
<li>Set CLR to 1.</li>
<li>Set A0-A1 and D0-D6 as required.</li>
<li>Set <span class=3D"overline">WR</span> to 0, so the data will be used, =
then set <span class=3D"overline">WR</span> to 1 again. According to the da=
ta sheet, the shortest possible write cycle is between <span class=3D"nowra=
p">60 ns</span> (at <span class=3D"nowrap">-40=C2=B0C</span>) and <span cla=
ss=3D"nowrap">90 ns</span> (at <span class=3D"nowrap">+85=C2=B0C</span>), d=
epending on the temperature.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Clear the display content</h3>
<p>Unlike the <a href=3D"https://arnowelzel.de/en/led-display-dl-2416-hdlo-=
2416">DL-2416</a>, <span class=3D"overline">CLR</span> must be set to 0 for=
 at least 15 ms to clear the current content of the display. The saved curs=
or positions are retained.</p>
<h3>Cursor control</h3>
<p>Writing of the cursor positions can be activated via the <span class=3D"=
overline">CU</span> input. In this case you specify the character position =
with A0 and A1 and with D0 whether the cursor (1) or the character stored t=
here (0) should appear at the specified position. With <span class=3D"overl=
ine">WR</span> the information is used.</p>
<p>With CUE you control whether the cursor symbol generally appears at the =
saved cursor positions (1) or not (0). A flashing cursor can be achieved by=
 regularly switching the CUE between 0 and 1.</p>
<h3>Controlling the brightness</h3>
<p>Unlike the <a href=3D"https://arnowelzel.de/en/led-display-dl-2416-hdlo-=
2416">DL-2416</a>, <span class=3D"overline">BL</span> is only described as =
an input to generally switch off the display. Brightness control via a PWM =
signal with at least 2.5 kHz is not described. Since the basic technology i=
s very similar, this should be possible in principle.</p>
<h3>Character set</h3>
<p>The character set covers all ASCII characters with the codes 32-127.</p>
<p><a href=3D"https://arnowelzel.de/en/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2023/11/d=
l3422-charset.png" data-lbwps-width=3D"1798" data-lbwps-height=3D"821" data=
-lbwps-srcsmall=3D"https://arnowelzel.de/en/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2023=
/11/dl3422-charset-400x183.png"><img loading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" cl=
ass=3D"alignnone size-large wp-image-6119" src=3D"https://arnowelzel.de/en/=
wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2023/11/dl3422-charset-800x365.png" alt=3D"DL-34=
22 character set" width=3D"800" height=3D"365"></a></p>
<h2>Practical experiments</h2>
<p>When it comes to power supply, you should keep in mind that USB alone is=
 usually not enough. In my experiemnts I used a bench power supply. The fol=
lowing video shows a demo using an Arduino micro. The source code is simila=
r to that used for the <a href=3D"https://arnowelzel.de/en/led-display-dl-2=
416-hdlo-2416">DL-2416</a>.</p>
